    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Do You Need Travel Insurance for a Boat Trip in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>16 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Not for the trip itself: a licensed boat operator in Portugal must hold personal accident and civil liability insurance, and a booking with free cancellation protects what you paid. You still want travel insurance for the holiday, because the EHIC or GHIC does not pay for private care, a flight home or a phone lost at sea.</p>

      <h2>What insurance must a boat operator in Madeira already have?</h2><p>Two policies, by law. Under Decree-Law 108/2009, the Portuguese law for tourist activity companies, a registered operator must hold personal accident insurance and civil liability insurance. Turismo de Portugal adds that both policies must list every activity the company runs. For boat operators, the boat's own insurance can stand in for the civil liability policy, as long as it covers all of those activities with at least the same capital.</p><p>In plain words: personal accident insurance is there for injuries that happen during the activity, and civil liability insurance is there if the operator is at fault for harm or damage. Both are about the trip. Neither is a holiday policy. They will not pay for your flight home, an illness that has nothing to do with the boat, or a camera that slips into the sea. That is the gap your own cover fills.</p><p>Chifbay belongs to CHIF&amp;CO, Lda, which holds maritime tourism registration RNAAT n.º 305/2026. Registration with Turismo de Portugal is the step where those insurance obligations come in, so a valid number is a good first sign.</p><h2>Does the EHIC or GHIC cover you in Madeira?</h2><p>Yes, for state healthcare. Madeira is part of Portugal and of the EU, so a European Health Insurance Card gives you medically necessary care in the public system on the same terms and at the same cost as people insured in Portugal. UK travellers get the same through the Global Health Insurance Card, and the UK government's advice for Portugal says so.</p><p>The card has hard limits, set out on the <a href="https://employment-social-affairs.ec.europa.eu/node/53_en" rel="noopener">European Commission's EHIC page</a>. It is not an alternative to travel insurance. It does not cover private healthcare, return flights to your home country or lost and stolen property. Treatment is not always free either: you pay whatever a local patient would pay.</p><h2>Who pays for what, case by case?</h2><p>Most worries about a boat trip fall into a handful of situations. Here is where each one usually lands.</p><table><thead><tr><th>What happens</th><th>Operator's insurance</th><th>EHIC or GHIC</th><th>Your travel insurance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>You are hurt during the trip</td><td>This is what the personal accident policy is for, within its limits</td><td>State treatment on local terms</td><td>Extra costs, such as private care or getting home</td></tr><tr><td>You fall ill on holiday, unrelated to the boat</td><td>No</td><td>State treatment on local terms</td><td>Yes, if your policy covers it</td></tr><tr><td>Your phone goes overboard</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Only with gadget cover that includes accidental loss</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Cancellations are the part that surprises people, because no insurance is needed for most of them. The trip's own price is protected by the booking rules:</p><ul><li><strong>The skipper calls it off because the sea is unsafe:</strong> you choose a new date or a full refund.</li><li><strong>You cancel more than 24 hours ahead:</strong> it costs nothing.</li><li><strong>You cannot go on the day itself:</strong> that is no longer a free cancellation, and travel insurance may help only if your reason is on its list.</li></ul><p>So with Chifbay, the weather risk sits with us, not with your insurer. The details are on our <a href="/practical">practical page with cancellation rules</a>.</p><h2>What about phones and cameras lost at sea?</h2><p>This is the claim most likely to come up on a boat, and the one most often excluded. Before you rely on any policy, check four things:</p><ol><li><strong>Is there gadget cover at all?</strong> Some basic policies have none, or only a low limit for valuables.</li><li><strong>Is accidental loss covered, or only theft?</strong> A phone dropped into deep water is lost, not stolen.</li><li><strong>What is the single item limit?</strong> A new phone or a camera body can be worth more than the cap.</li><li><strong>What is the excess?</strong> On a mid-range phone, the excess can swallow most of the payout.</li></ol><p>Your home insurance may also cover possessions away from home, so look there before you pay twice. The simplest protection is not to need the phone much. Every one of our trips includes camera photos, a 360° video shot on an Insta360 and, weather permitting, drone footage from above, which is why we suggest keeping your own phone in the bag at the riskiest moments. Straps, pouches and what to do if salt water gets in are covered in our piece on <a href="/posts/keep-your-phone-and-camera-safe-on-a-boat-trip">protecting a phone or camera on the water</a>.</p><h2>Is swimming or paddle boarding covered by travel insurance?</h2><p>Read the activities list. Policies differ on what counts as a normal holiday activity and what needs an add-on. Swimming from a boat and paddle boarding with a skipper nearby are gentle by most standards, but the wording of your policy is what counts, not common sense. If paddle boarding is not mentioned, ask your insurer before you go, in writing.</p><h2>Who really needs extra cover?</h2><p>Honestly, the boat trip changes little. If you already travel with a good policy, it probably does the job. Extra care makes sense in three cases:</p><ul><li><strong>Health conditions.</strong> Declare them properly, or a claim may fail. Ask your doctor about the trip too.</li><li><strong>Expensive kit.</strong> A professional camera or drone usually needs specific cover.</li><li><strong>A tight travel plan.</strong> If missing a flight would cost a lot, make sure delay and medical repatriation are in the policy. The EHIC and GHIC never pay for repatriation.</li></ul><p>You do not need a special "boat trip insurance". You need to know what your own policy says, and to save one number: 112 is the emergency number anywhere in Portugal. For what happens to the booking itself when the sea turns, read <a href="/posts/what-happens-to-a-boat-trip-in-madeira-in-bad-weather">what happens to a Madeira boat trip in bad weather</a>. Prices for both trips are set per boat, not per person, and you will find them on <a href="/experiences">the overview of our two trips</a>.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is a boat trip covered by normal travel insurance?</h3><p>Check two parts of your policy: the activities list, for swimming and paddle boarding, and the gadget section, if you bring an expensive phone or camera.</p><h3>Does the EHIC work in Madeira?</h3><p>Yes. Madeira is part of Portugal, so the EHIC, and the UK GHIC, give access to medically necessary state healthcare on local terms. They do not cover private care, flights home or lost items.</p><h3>Who pays if I am hurt on the boat?</h3><p>Licensed operators in Portugal must hold personal accident and civil liability insurance. Your own travel insurance covers what falls outside them, such as private care or getting home.</p><h3>Do I need insurance in case the trip is cancelled for weather?</h3><p>Not with Chifbay. A trip the skipper calls off for safety is moved or refunded in full, as you prefer, and cancelling yourself is free until 24 hours before departure.</p>

      <blockquote>Insurance covers the accident. The booking rules cover the weather. Your own policy covers the phone, if you read it first.</blockquote>
    </article>
